      "content": "Considering that common pests and physical causes have been ruled out, I would now recommend checking for potential chemical or environmental problems. It's possible that a recent change to the greenhouse environment—like new fertilizers, pots, tools, or even a new pest control product—might be causing phytotoxicity to the leaves. Carefully observe any new elements introduced to the greenhouse in the last few weeks. Phytotoxicity can cause the leaves to develop spots, burn, or otherwise become damaged. Moreover, nesting or hibernating wildlife such as small rodents or birds might be hiding in your greenhouse and disturbing the soil around your plants, indirectly causing damage to the roots and leading to the observed 'holes'. Consider using motion-activated cameras or installing small traps to catch any wildlife. Reassess your greenhouse care regimen, ensuring that all items are clean and sanitized to prevent any potential chemical reactions. Make adjustments accordingly and reevaluate after a few days. Remember that meticulous and frequent monitoring over a period will be key to identifying the exact cause.",
